Well, so you should all have your Ripple open and see these three arrows and a blinking cursor to indicate that you can now start typing into it.

02:12.000 --> 02:18.000
So I mentioned earlier that Python is a dynamic language.

02:18.000 --> 02:23.000
And we defined some variables earlier on when we were exploring the Ripple.

02:23.000 --> 02:32.000
If you come from a language like Java, you will notice that I didn't have to specify the type when I instantiated this variable.

02:32.000 --> 02:36.000
I didn't have to tell Python, hey, this is an integer.

02:36.000 --> 02:41.000
I could just define whatever variable I wanted.

02:41.000 --> 02:47.000
And the type doesn't need to be consistent throughout the execution of your code.

02:47.000 --> 02:53.000
This could be a number in one instance. I can change it to a string in another.

02:53.000 --> 03:05.000
This is something that you want to be very mindful of because it can introduce a lot of subtle bugs.

03:05.000 --> 03:11.000
And because Python is a dynamic language, naming variables is very, very important.

03:11.000 --> 03:14.000
So you want to be as explicit as possible.

03:14.000 --> 03:23.000
If you've moved down from the learning phase, you really want to avoid having any single variable variable names where you're not really sure what they do.

03:23.000 --> 03:26.000
Because you're going to end up in a world of pain afterwards.

03:26.000 --> 03:34.000
So be as descriptive in naming your variables as possible.

03:34.000 --> 03:43.000
Now, in Python, convention says that your variable should be lowercase and separated with underscores.

03:43.000 --> 03:49.000
That is called snake casing, which I think is pretty cute.

03:49.000 --> 03:54.000
Snake case naming your variables and snake case is convention in Python.

03:54.000 --> 04:05.000
So try to follow that as much as possible regardless of what the convention is in the programming language that you're coming to Python from.

04:05.000 --> 04:11.000
In Python variable names cannot start with special symbols or numbers.

04:11.000 --> 04:21.000
So if I try to name a variable like this, I'm going to get the syntax error saying that this is invalid syntax.

04:21.000 --> 04:31.000
There are some variable names that Python will allow you to use, but you really, really shouldn't.

04:31.000 --> 04:41.000
And these are special keywords in Python. They are built in functions and this is something that might bite you as your learning Python.

04:41.000 --> 04:58.000
But I think you're, you'll quickly start to recognize when this is happening and when we start working in files, those, those keywords will be a different color and so you'll be able to recognize them a little bit more easily.

04:58.000 --> 05:05.000
So some obvious, some obvious keywords are going to result in syntax errors.

05:05.000 --> 05:16.000
If I try to name a variable, if that's a invalid syntax, syntax error, because that's the definition of an if statement.

05:16.000 --> 05:21.000
But here's a really, really subtle bug that bitten me so many times when I was learning Python.

05:21.000 --> 05:36.000
So in Python, one of the ways that you can declare a list because in Python everything is an object is by calling the list constructor and that just returns an empty list.

05:36.000 --> 05:39.000
Will this, let's do this.

05:39.000 --> 05:43.000
Is this a syntax error in Python?

05:43.000 --> 05:46.000
No.

05:46.000 --> 05:49.000
No, it is not.

05:49.000 --> 06:03.000
Now, if we try to make a new list again using the list constructor, we're going to get an error that says that stir, stir meaning string object is not callable.

06:03.000 --> 06:09.000
Because what's actually happening under the hood is that Python is trying to do this.

06:09.000 --> 06:15.000
You've just made the, the list constructor into a string.

06:15.000 --> 06:21.000
And this is one way of having very, very subtle annoying errors.

06:21.000 --> 06:32.000
So don't name, don't name your variables, things like int, stir, list, et cetera.

06:32.000 --> 06:44.000
If you're not sure while you're working on the REPL, you can type in one of these keywords just right in the REPL and it will come back with class in this case class int.

06:44.000 --> 06:48.000
That means that this is one of those built ints in Python.

